Z||000000||GT Bastard||05-27-2000||05:57 PM||||Found this thread on the v6 thirdgen f-body board.... What a bunch of BS<BR> <A HREF="http://www.umount.com/thirdgen/techboard/Forum10/HTML/000634.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.umount.com/thirdgen/techboard/Forum10/HTML/000634.html</A> ||||reg||12
Z||000001||terryk||05-27-2000||06:12 PM||||I read the post and all of the qualifiers are there.<P>HIGH RPM. HP increases with higher RPM. The torque is way down, but that's not what they were arguing.<P>Heck, 400 HP possible if you can keep it together at 11,000 RPM.<P>Welcome to Honda.....||||reg||1
Z||000002||DaRkLoRD||05-27-2000||07:06 PM||||I don't know a lot about engine performance.. how do you get an engine to rev up to 9,000 RPM, when stock it only pulled 6k?<P>what kind of mods would be required?||||reg||1
Z||000003||Richard Parnell||05-27-2000||07:29 PM||||Well, engines have changed a lot (understatement) since when I used to work on flatheads and 327 Chevies and such, where there was spark, gas and brakes and not much else.  But I was reading through some car parts catalogs the other day and noticed an ignition module that replaces the stock one in the distributor.  The ad stated that with this module you could rev. to 12,000 rpm.  As to whether the engine will stay together, I don't know .  You can always make most motors over rev, especially if they are not computer controlled, depends on the parts for it to stay together.||||reg||1
Z||000004||GT Bastard||05-27-2000||08:07 PM||||Well, what allows an engine to stay together?  Forged crank and pistons, full roller rockers, roller cam, roller timing chain, beefed fuel delivery and ignition, etc... I'm guessing there was never a 60 degree block with 4-bolt main caps, right?  What do you think we could get a 2.8 to rev to?||||reg||1
Z||000005||batboy||05-27-2000||08:51 PM||||The main stumbling block to high RPM with a normal American single cam, pushrod engine is the valve train. All those import screamers have dual overhead cams and light weight valves. Another thing to consider is that the 3.4 block was significantly beefed up over the 2.8 block, there is a reason for that, which is the 2.8 block is maxed out for the amount of power it can reliably withstand. You want high revs, swap in the 3.4 TDC engine.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Sillhouette.com||05-27-2000||09:29 PM||||Hey gents and laidy<P>My fiero revs up to 6000 rpm without valve floating.  This was achieved by custom pistons, roller rocker crane cam larger intake larger throttle body and shorty headers all care of RSM.   (that's also what is in their race car)  You should hear the thing whine at those high revs  it's sweeet.<P>Later||||reg||1
Z||000007||TOM||05-27-2000||09:47 PM||||My motor cycle engine revs to 11,000 safley. because everything is small and light. you can also get a rotary to rev way high. I know we did it. alot.  There is no valve train problems because there is no valve train. ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Phaeton||05-27-2000||10:58 PM||||My 2.8 block is  four bolt main bearing, separate oil gallies for lifters and bearing and if the rods were upgraded it would be good past the seven thousand RPM it is good for now.<BR>I raced small block chevies with the same valvetrain I'm using now (Yes it is tapped for 7/16" studs) and nine thousand just wore out rings really fast.<P>250 hp at eight grand is pretty silly for a street driven car IMHO but to each his own. ||||reg||12
Z||000009||Shiner||05-27-2000||11:15 PM||||The stock 2.8 (with getrag) in my '87 GT will rev up to 6k with no problem at all, but after like 5500 rpm, the cam and the ignition dont have much more to give.  But, IMHO, this is the best setupd for a STREET car.  The stock cam makes lots of low-mid power, which is the range you are driving in most of the time.  <P> Of course, this will all be monkeyed with once I get my hands on a spare engine block, a decent wad of cash, and a Garret T3  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000010||HYPTNOTiSE||05-29-2000||02:47 PM||||It is possible to make 250hp with a 2.8.  This guy is using gen III heads, unbelievable heads IMHO.  Roller rockers, roller cam, custom pistons, rods, and crank.  This engine could sure pull to 9000rpm and stay together. But for how long.  This car could be street driven, But not reliably every day.  Theres no possible way to make 250hp with the stock computer.  The computer wouldn't put up with the high duration needed to make this car pull hard to 7500-8000 rpm.  Personally i'm sick of all these "fake" hp claims in magazines and on these lists.  People in these magazine crank the boost up to 20-30psi, and tell you thats how much hp they're car makes. It's BS.  There is no way for a car to sustain that all the time.  My 3.2 has custom forged venoilia pistons, SBC rods, with tapered wristpins, high polished, Arp bolts, My crank is forged, with billet splayed mains on 2&3.  My cam is the crane 204/216 .454 .480(with 1.6 rockers) with an LDA of 112.  Thats the largest cam that i believe the stock computer can handle. I invested about 1500 into my heads, doing some of the work myself. This motor should make 225+ hp.  That is reliable horsepower.  That is all day hp, street driven.  I'll probably shift out of 1st at 6700rpm, and shift out of 2nd and 3rd at 6100-6200 rpm.  I think my engine should stay together at high rpm (7000+), but theres no point in reving that high.  The cam isn't going let the motor keep making power. <P>P.s. Someone posted earlier that they have 4 bolt mains. Could you please respond with how you did so? Thanks!  ||||reg||4
Z||000011||terryk||05-29-2000||10:59 PM||||Possible vs practical. Yep, that says it all.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Phaeton||05-30-2000||12:35 AM||||Chevy dealers used to sell an all aluminum block for race use, nodular iron dry liner good to 91mm, triple oil gallery, four bolt main, mounts for transverse or longitudinal, starter either side,modified bearing saddle oiling, crank trigger mount, plus just overall stronger casting.<BR>Another member posted he had got one also but they are discontinued now.<P>No dyno, but am guessing about 190 horsepower, the engine is underbuilt for what it is capable of.  It won't go pop if I miss a shift though.||||reg||12
Z||000013||terryk||05-30-2000||02:45 PM||||I don't think the AL block is still available, but I know someone had several they were selling.<P>New, they were around $2500. Get a Chevy Power book. It lists the part number. I'll dig it out if someone is interested. Frankly, I think it's a waste of money for the street.||||reg||1
Z||000014||Phaeton||05-30-2000||04:50 PM||||Absolutely not necessary for street performance, I catch more crap for spending US5000.00 in parts for under 200 horsepower.  However, my ass-o-meter is hooked up and says the 100 pounds saved with all aluminum verses all iron translates into money well spent, plus it will take anoter two cleanup bores, all the way to 3.3L.<P>To each his own, this is mine.||||reg||12
Z||000015||lowCG||05-31-2000||06:15 PM||||I must be tired of messing with micrometers,machine $hop$ that don't do what they said they did,poor running at low speed,tuning nightmares,because I gave in and let GM do the tweaking this time.<BR>Hope GM wasn't jivin' me with the hp figures on the 3.4TDC motor!||||reg||1
Z||000016||Shaun41178||06-01-2000||04:31 AM||||I am guessing Phaeton has an aluminum block.  I do as well.  I plan on using the stock iron heads though(bummer really)<P>Phaeton: What intake you  use to use the aluminum heads?  Custom fab your own or did you just use Stock intake pieces that came with the al head engines?  if you used the facotry intake of the aluminum head engines, how did you solve the throttle body linkage mounting.routing?<P>Did you just go to a DIS system?  Could you explain your setup a bit more cause i would much rather use aluminum heads to give me an all aluminum engine like you.<P>Email me with details or post here.  <P>Can you supply any pics at all?  Thanks<P>Shaun||||reg||1
